## 🎯 Calling Conventions & Stack Discipline

Stack corruption and crash `0xC0000005` (Access Violation) almost always stem from mismatched calling conventions.

### 32-bit x86 Calling Conventions (Crucial for Older Engines)

| Convention | `this` Pointer | Argument Passing | Stack Cleanup | Assembly Signature |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **`__cdecl`** | Stack | Right-to-Left on Stack | **Caller** cleans | `add esp, 0x10` after `call` |
| **`__stdcall`** | Stack | Right-to-Left on Stack | **Callee** cleans | `ret 0x10` at end of function |
| **`__thiscall`** | **`ecx` register** | Right-to-Left on Stack | **Callee** cleans | `mov ecx, pObj` then `call`, ends with `ret 0x08` |
| **`__fastcall`** | `ecx` (arg1), `edx` (arg2) | Remaining on Stack | **Callee** cleans | `ret 0x08` |

> [!CAUTION]
> **Stack Imbalance Warning:** If a 32-bit function ending in `ret 0x08` (`__stdcall` or `__thiscall`) is called as `__cdecl`, the callee pops 8 bytes and the caller adds 8 bytes to `esp`, popping 16 bytes total. The next `ret` will jump into garbage memory.

### 64-bit Microsoft x64 ABI (Modern Engines & Plugins)

* **Integer / Pointer Arguments:** Passed in `rcx`, `rdx`, `r8`, `r9` in order.
* **Floating Point Arguments:** Passed in `xmm0`, `xmm1`, `xmm2`, `xmm3`.
* **5th+ Arguments:** Pushed onto the stack (right-to-left).
* **Shadow Space (Home Space):** Caller **MUST** allocate 32 bytes (`0x20`) on the stack before making any function call, even if the function takes 0 arguments.
* **16-Byte Stack Alignment:** The stack pointer `rsp` must be 16-byte aligned before any `call` instruction.

